{"name":"Copper Brown","hex":"#9a6051","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"copper","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A reddish-brown metallic chemical element (symbol Cu) with the atomic number 29; also, the metal made up of this element.","The reddish-brown colour of copper (etymology 1, noun sense 1)."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of copper (etymology 1, noun sense 1).","Having the reddish-brown colour of copper."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/copper"},{"term":"brown","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A colour like that of chocolate or coffee.","One of the colour balls used in snooker, with a value of 4 points."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a brown color/colour.","Gloomy."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/brown"}],"entries":[{"title":"Copper","blocks":[{"text":"Copper is a chemical element; it has symbol Cu and atomic number 29. It is a soft, malleable, and ductile metal with very high thermal and electrical conductivity. A freshly exposed surface of pure copper has a pinkish-orange color. Copper is used as a conductor of heat and electricity, as a building material, and as a constituent of various metal alloys, such as sterling silver used in jewelry, cupronickel used to make marine hardware and coins, and constantan used in strain gauges and thermocouples for temperature measurement."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/NatCopper.jpg/330px-NatCopper.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Copper","wikidata":"Q753"},{"title":"Brown","blocks":[{"text":"Brown is a color. It can be thought as a darker, typically desaturated shade of orange, and can often be produced by combining red and yellow with another color, namely blue."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Espresso-roasted_coffee_beans.jpg/330px-Espresso-roasted_coffee_beans.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brown","wikidata":"Q47071"},{"title":"Copper (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Copper is a reddish brown color that resembles the metal copper. The first recorded use of copper as a color name in English was in 1594."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Copper-18706.jpg/330px-Copper-18706.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Copper_(color)","wikidata":"Q2722041"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Cu-Scheibe.JPG/500px-Cu-Scheibe.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Cu-Scheibe.JPG/1280px-Cu-Scheibe.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Cu-Scheibe.JPG/500px-Cu-Scheibe.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A copper disc (99.95% pure) made by continuous casting; etched to reveal crystallites","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACu-Scheibe.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Molten_copper_in_bright_sunlight.gif/250px-Molten_copper_in_bright_sunlight.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/06/Molten_copper_in_bright_sunlight.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Molten_copper_in_bright_sunlight.gif/250px-Molten_copper_in_bright_sunlight.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Copper just above its melting point keeps its pink luster color when enough light outshines the orange incandescence c